        "content": "<p>Quick diagnosis before I have to run: <code>set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false in</code>  fixes the <code>simp</code> and then</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TopCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Homotopy</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>works. This hints at a mismatch in implicit arguments that <code>simp</code> is not seeing through (there is a lot of def-eq abuse going on here, because of the usage of <code>TopCat.Homotopy.h</code> which brings in the monoidal category structure of <code>TopCat</code> and through which <code>simp</code> can't see through).</p>\n<p>The reason why extracting the <code>test</code> lemma behaves differently is that the implicit arguments are re-synthesized differently, so there is no more mismatch and <code>simp</code> works.</p>\n<p>One way to make this less painful would be to define a separate <code>TopCat.Homotopy.refl</code> and a lemma characterizing its <code>h</code> field. Then staying in the monoidal category language instead of descending to elements would probably make the proof much better.</p>",
        "id": 584251239,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Merten",
        "timestamp": 1775661244
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You may be able to fix this by copying the API from <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/2ee41a0315994fc1b24421078d4011e19745a19b/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Monoidal/Types/Basic.lean\">here</a> to the file defining the monoidal structure on <code>TopCat</code>. I mean creating the lemma <code>tensorObj_def</code>, turning it on as local simp in that file and applying <code>dsimp%</code> to the apply lemmas</p>",
        "id": 584251870,
        "sender_full_name": "Dagur Asgeirsson",
        "timestamp": 1775661418
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ahh thanks these are great suggestions!</p>",
        "id": 584307383,
        "sender_full_name": "Jacob Scharmberg",
        "timestamp": 1775679435
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I just want to mention, that for diagnosing issues with <code>set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false in</code> the new command<br>\n<code>#defeq_abuse in</code> (from mathlib) might be useful for you to put above your failing simp.<br>\nIt will tell you likely candidates that are defeq at full transparency but not defeq at the currently used transparency, which prevent your simp from working</p>",
        "id": 584459837,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Roos",
        "timestamp": 1775742931
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you, that is also very useful!</p>",
